(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-05"] = {"8.1":{"s":"Hydrogen is the lightest and simplest element, with one proton and one electron. It is placed at the top of the periodic table because it resembles both the alkali metals and the halogens, yet fits neither group fully. It has three isotopes: protium, deuterium and tritium. Dihydrogen, H₂, is made in the laboratory from a metal and a dilute acid, and in industry from steam and hydrocarbons. It is a colourless gas that burns and is a good reducing agent.","w":["The three isotopes of hydrogen are protium (no neutron), deuterium (one neutron) and tritium (two neutrons); only tritium is radioactive.","Hydrogen has the same one electron in its outer shell as the alkali metals, but a very high ionisation enthalpy, unlike them."]},"8.2":{"s":"Group 1 elements, the alkali metals, and group 2 elements, the alkaline earth metals, are the s-block metals. Their atoms have one or two electrons in the outer s orbital, so they form M⁺ and M²⁺ ions. They are soft, reactive metals, and the reactivity rises on going down the group. Their compounds are mostly ionic. Lithium and beryllium behave unlike the rest of their groups, and each resembles the second element of the next group, a diagonal relationship.","w":["Reactivity increases down both groups: potassium reacts more vigorously with water than lithium.","Lithium is the odd one in group 1, as beryllium is in group 2: lithium resembles magnesium, beryllium resembles aluminium."]},"8.3":{"s":"Some compounds of the s-block elements are of great use. Washing soda, sodium carbonate, is used for softening water and in cleaning. Caustic soda, sodium hydroxide, is a strong base used in making soap and paper. Calcium carbonate occurs as limestone and marble and is the source of lime and cement. Hydrogen peroxide is a bleach and a disinfectant. Lithium aluminium hydride is a strong reducing agent used in organic chemistry.","w":["Washing soda is Na₂CO₃·10H₂O, while baking soda is NaHCO₃: do not mix them up.","Hydrogen peroxide can act as both an oxidising and a reducing agent."]}};
